    tmk:tier 3 .

tmka:GR-0532 a tmk:AuthoredAssertion ;
    tmk:assertionObject tmkc:GC-0133 ;
    tmk:assertionPredicate skos:related ;
    tmk:assertionSubject tmkc:GC-0096 ;
    tmk:authoredBy "gpt-6.1-sol" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Manual expressly connects mandatory deferment with referral of certification trade mark rules to the ACCC. No more specific listed predicate accurately captures the relationship between the procedural step and the receiving body."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 9.4e-01 ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether the ACCC's role should instead be modelled through a separate referral-event concept."@en-AU ;
    tmk:extractionMethod "agent_authoring" ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GR-0532" ;
    tmk:isStale false ;
    tmk:modality "must" ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourceContentHash "sha256:0ea4f34864b31f467d6bc938957878f89da62f4fa2801ffccaedc33b32f57c32" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part17/2/2/2> ;
    tmk:spanEnd 1217 ;
    tmk:spanStart 885 ;
    tmk:supportingText "Where, in the case of a certification trade mark, the Registrar sends a copy of the rules governing use of the certification trade mark to the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission (ACCC). The Registrar has no discretion as to whether deferment is granted - they must defer acceptance in this circumstance (subreg 4.13(3))." ;
    tmk:tier 2 .

tmka:GR-0533 a tmk:AuthoredAssertion ;
    tmk:assertionObject tmkc:GC-0139 ;
    tmk:assertionPredicate skos:related ;
    tmk:assertionSubject tmkc:GC-0096 ;
    tmk:authoredBy "gpt-6.1-sol" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Manual connects deferment with the availability of an appeal to a prescribed court following an adverse decision. The court is the potential appeal forum rather than the body directed to defer acceptance, so no more specific listed predicate fits."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 9.3e-01 ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether 'will defer' warrants the normative modality 'must' for this statement of Manual practice."@en-AU ;
    tmk:extractionMethod "agent_authoring" ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GR-0533" ;
    tmk:isStale false ;
    tmk:modality "must" ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourceContentHash "sha256:32928d8cb21e48d4445a733a7dbc9f183771c72bedfdc3d33059ea0dda111e4c" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part17/5/5/1> ;
    tmk:spanEnd 297 ;
    tmk:spanStart 6 ;
    tmk:supportingText "Where the Registrar or the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission (ACCC) makes a decision adverse to an applicant, and that decision is appealable under the Act to a prescribed court, or may be reviewed by the ART, the Registrar will defer acceptance of the application for 2 months." ;
    tmk:tier 2 .

tmka:GR-0534 a tmk:AuthoredAssertion ;
    tmk:assertionObject tmkc:GC-0096 ;
    tmk:assertionPredicate skos:related ;
    tmk:assertionSubject tmkc:GC-0098 ;
    tmk:authoredBy "gpt-6.1-sol" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_inferred" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Manual connects the acceptance deadline that determines lapse with deferment when an examination hearing is requested. This supports a procedural connection, but I am unsure whether the contextual link is sufficiently clear; the passage does not expressly specify deferment's effect on lapse."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 6.8e-01 ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether the contextual discussion supports a relationship beyond merely mentioning both procedures."@en-AU ;
    tmk:extractionMethod "agent_authoring" ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GR-0534" ;
    tmk:isStale false ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourceContentHash "sha256:2f2be55cdd83aa13ccdb84eb727a1a7f176a8173346d737965a51ec0dbf34674" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part16/1/1/1> ;
    tmk:spanEnd 624 ;
    tmk:spanStart 0 ;
    tmk:supportingText "The legislation states that \"an application lapses if it is not accepted within the prescribed period or within that period as extended in accordance with the regulations\" (section 37). The prescribed period is 15 months from the date of the first report, or 15 months from the date of any further report that raises new grounds for rejection of the application. This is the basic time period within which an applicant for a trade mark is expected to finalise the application to the point of acceptance. If an applicant requests an ex parte hearing on an exam matter, the Registrar will defer acceptance of that application." ;
    tmk:tier 2 .

